WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Billionaire to Dole Out $9M in 'Serious Social Experiment'

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:18.000
It is said that money doesn't buy happiness, but a Japanese billionaire thinks it might at least cheer some people up.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Just a couple of weeks after Zozo Town founder Yusaku Maezawa donated more than $18 million to a Japanese city hit hard by a typhoon, the fashion-minded entrepreneur is back in the news with another giveaway.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:48.000
According to Reuters news agency, Maezawa ponied up $9 million and distributed bundles of $9,000 to 1,000 lucky recipients.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The people were chosen at random after they retweeted his New Year’s Day tweet on the matter.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:10.000
"It's a serious social experiment," Maezawa said in an accompanying YouTube video, adding he hopes this initiative piques the interest of those in academia and economics.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Maezawa has always been curious about the overall concept of universal basic income, also known as UBI.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
This was an idea often floated by former US presidential candidate Andrew Yang.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.000
UBI is when the citizens of a nation receive a regular, guaranteed payment from the government.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Maezawa is interested in how a similar idea would affect people's overall well-being and happiness. 

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:54.000
An economist tells Reuters that what Maezawa is doing is somewhat different than the Universal Basic Income idea. 

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Unlike UBI, it's just a one-time payment to each recipient. 

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Also, at the moment in Japan, people can still find a job quite easily due to its tight labor market. 

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Still, Maezawa wants to see how his "winners" react. 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
"What happens if someone who can't help themselves with their own power is given money... to rebuild their lives?" he tweeted. 

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.000
"Can they turn their life around?"
